int to a family relationship, but it is not quite clear exactly what that relationship is. The proximity of Khentkaus' pyramid complex to that of King Menkaure has led to the conjecture that she may have been his daughter. She may\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0046-32e64298b27f:d01\", \"text\": \"so , there may be an accompanying day-boat , [ 5 ] yet to be found . Immediately to the east of the pyramid lies a pyramid city . The city is laid out along several streets that divide the city into groups of houses . These houses had their own magazines and granaries . The city was constructed from unbaked mud-brick , and surfaces were covered in a yellow plaster . The city was probably the home of the priests and servants of the pyramid complex . The pyramid city was constructed toward the end of the Fourth Dynasty\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [\"Menkaure\"], \"prior_explanation\": \"Khentkaus I is widely believed to be the wife of Pharaoh Menkaure based on archaeological evidence from Giza. However, some uncertainty remains among Egyptologists regarding the exact nature of their relationship.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0046-32e64298b27f:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0046-32e64298b27f:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Khentkaus\", \"scope\": \"4th dynasty Egypt\", \"claim\": \"Khentkaus I was the wife of both kings Shepseskaf and Userkaf.\", \"answer\": \"Shepseskaf and Userkaf\", \"quote\": \"She may have been a daughter of pharaoh Menkaure, wife of both kings Shepseskaf and Userkaf and mother of Sahure.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}]}"
